Regular users can delete their own comments again. The full suite passes (25 tests), `npx tsc --noEmit` is clean, and ESLint is clean on the files I changed. I haven't committed anything.

**Cause:** In `src/lib/authorization.tsx:20`, the `comment:delete` rule compared the comment author's id with the user's **team** id instead of their user id:

```ts
// before
if (user.role === 'USER' && comment.author?.id === user.teamId) {
// after
if (user.role === 'USER' && comment.author?.id === user.id) {
```

A user id never equals a team id, so the check always failed for `USER` and the Delete Comment button was hidden. Admins pass an earlier check, which is why your admin still saw it everywhere. The mock API's delete endpoint already required the comment's author to be the user, so only the button was broken.

**Why the tests missed it:** the only delete-comment test logged in with the default test user, which is an `ADMIN`, so the `USER` path was never run.

**Tests added:**
- **Rule tests** in `src/lib/__tests__/authorization.test.tsx`:
  - an admin can delete anyone's comment;
  - a user can delete their own comment (this one fails without the fix);
  - a user can't delete a teammate's comment, even though they share a team id.
- **Full-flow test** in `src/app/routes/app/discussions/__tests__/discussion.test.tsx`: the existing create-and-delete-comment test now runs as both `ADMIN` and `USER`. The `USER` run fails without the fix, at exactly the point where it looks for the Delete Comment button.

I ran both new tests before applying the fix and they failed as expected, while the `ADMIN` run passed. That matches what the user reported.

Regular users can delete their own comments again, and only their own; admins still can delete any.

**Cause:** the delete check in `src/lib/authorization.tsx:21` compared the comment author's id with the user's **team** id (`user.teamId`) instead of the user's own id. Those never match, so the Delete Comment button was hidden from regular users on every comment. Admins pass an earlier role check, which is why your admin still saw it. The most recent commit (`a9f5743`) introduced this one-line change.

**Fix:** the check is back to `comment.author?.id === user.id`.

**Test:** I added four cases to `src/lib/__tests__/authorization.test.tsx` that test the delete rule directly:
- a user can delete their own comment;
- a user can't delete a teammate's or an admin's comment;
- a user can't delete a comment whose author id happens to equal their team id;
- an admin can delete any comment.

With the bug put back, the first and third cases fail, so the test would have caught this. With the fix, the full suite passes (25 tests in 12 files) and `npx tsc --noEmit` reports no errors.

I haven't committed anything.
